      "column_index": 1,
      "header_id": "C15",
      "x_range": [
        473.5,
        2170.9
      ],
      "section_title": "List of Lists = Tree",
      "section_number": 1,
      "teaching_text": "1- List of Lists = Tree\r\n\r\n- While a List contains simple items, a Tree contains Lists.\r\n\r\n- The \"Partition List\" component produces many partitions from one List.\r\n\r\n\r\nEach one of these partitions is a List.\r\nThe \"Partition List\" produces a Tree, which is a List of Lists.\r\n\r\n- The Wire Display of a Tree is a dashed double line.\r\n\r\n- A Tree is composed of Branches. Each Branch is a List.\r\n\r\n- Many components are related to Trees Data structures and can be found under the Tab \"Sets\", then \"Tree\".\r\n\r\n- Like items inside a List, the ordering of Branches inside a Tree also starts with a 0.\r\n\r\n- In Grasshopper, the order of Branches is represented as number inside brackets. For example, the order of the first Branch of any Tree is {0}  (with brackets).\r\nLikewise, the order of the first item of any List is 0  (without any brackets).\r\n\r\n- Knowing this, realize that any List is actually a Tree of only one Branch! And the List itself is represented as {0}.",
      "teaching_panel_id": "C382",
      "additional_teaching_panels": [
        {
          "id": "C384",
          "text": "2- Flattenning\r\n\r\n- \"Flattenning\" is a data restructuing concept in Grasshopper that brings any Tree to a basic List of items, getting rid of all branches.\r\n\r\n- This command is often very useful and we will see how to use it.\r\n\r\n- Type \"Flatten\" and choose \"Flatten Tree\".\r\n\r\n- Use the \"Panel\" to see what is happening with the data structure before and after flattenning."
        }
      ],
